            <ArticleTitle>Antibiotic Killing through Incomplete DNA Repair.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0966-842X(17)30255-X</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.tim.2017.11.006</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Two recent studies show that incomplete repair of DNA damage due to oxidized nucleotides is crucial for reactive oxygen species (ROS)-related antimicrobial lethality. Using widely different experimental approaches they both reach the same conclusions on the role of downstream ROS production in cell killing upon exposure to bactericidal antimicrobials.</AbstractText>
